1. Nf3 d5 2. c4 d4 3. g3 Nc6 4. Bg2 e5 (4... Bg4 { Same idea applies to Bf4, but we start with the queen: (3... Bf5 5. b4 Nxb4 6. Qa4+ Nc6 7. Ne5 Bd7 8. Nxc6 bxc6 9. Ba3) } 5. b4 Nxb4 { If black doesn't take - we'll likely play b5. } 6. Ne5 Bd7 7. Qb3 e6 8. a3 Nc6 9. Nxd7 Kxd7 10. Qxb7 { [%cal Gg8e7,Gb7b5,Gb7b3,Gd2d3] }) 5. d3 Nf6 (5... Bb4+ 6. Bd2 a5 (6... Bxd2+ 7. Nbxd2 Nf6 8. b4 Nxb4 (8... Qe7 { Black spots that the b-pawn shouldn't be taken and defends the e-pawn witth the queen... it's still dubious however } 9. b5 Nd8 10. a4 O-O 11. O-O c6 12. Re1 Bg4 13. e3) (8... a6 { Black tries to stop b5... } 9. b5 axb5 10. cxb5 Nb4 11. Qb3 Qe7 12. O-O Be6 13. Nc4 Nd7 14. a3 Nd5 15. Nfxe5 { It looks like black will have scary discoveries... but taking our rook is a mistake: (15... Nxe5 16. Nxe5 Ne3 17. Qb2 Nxf1 18. Bxb7 Rb8 19. Nc6) } { [%cal Gd7e5,Gc4e5,Bd5e3,Bb3b2] }) 9. Qa4+ Nc6 10. Nxe5 Bd7 11. Nxd7 Qxd7 12. O-O O-O 13. Rab1 { Black can't hold on to the b-pawn: (13... Nd8 14. Qxd7 Nxd7 15. Bxb7)(13... Qc8 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Qxc6) }) 7. Bxb4 axb4 8. O-O Nf6 9. Nbd2 O-O 10. a3 bxa3 11. Rxa3 Rxa3 12. bxa3 Qe7 13. Qa4 Bd7 14. Rb1 b6 15. Ne1 Na5 16. Qb4) (5... f5 { This f5 variation is not as dangerous as the original black one - we have time to castle earlier. } 6. O-O { As black we'd play a3/a6 here. 
As white, we can get away with castling. } 6... Nf6 (6... e4 7. dxe4 fxe4 8. Ng5 Nf6 9. Nd2 { We could take the pawn already - but we'd rather recapture with a knight. } { [%cal Gg5e4,Gd2e4,Rg2e4] } 9... e3 10. Nde4 exf2+ 11. Rxf2 Be7 12. Qc2 { Setting up some sneaky traps if black plays h6 to chase the knight. } { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gc2g6,Rh7h6,Ge4f6] }) 7. b4 e4 (7... Bxb4 8. Nxe5 Nxe5 9. Qa4+ Nc6 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. Qxb4 Qd6 12. Ba3) 8. dxe4 fxe4 9. Ng5 Bxb4 (9... Bf5 10. b5 Ne5 11. Nd2 e3 12. fxe3 dxe3 13. Rxf5 { [%cal Gf5e5] }) 10. a3 Bc5 11. Nd2 e3 12. Nde4 Nxe4 13. Nxe4 exf2+ 14. Rxf2) (5... Bg4 6. h3 Bh5 7. Qb3 Rb8 8. Nxe5 Nxe5 9. Qb5+ Nc6 10. Qxh5) (5... Bd6 6. O-O Nge7 7. e3 { Going a3 here isn't worth it - black is likely to have studies this and we'll be worse after a3 a5. } 7... O-O (7... dxe3 8. fxe3 O-O { [%cal Gf3g5,Gg5e4] } 9. Ng5 { Heading for e4 } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gg5e4] } 9... f5 { But black stops us } { [%cal Gc4c5,Gd6c5,Gd1b3] } 10. c5 Bxc5 11. Qb3+ Kh8 12. Nf7+ Rxf7 13. Qxf7 Nb4 14. Nc3 { Making room for the rook on b1. We'll loose the pawn on e3 but the position is equal.

Likely continuation: (14... Nc2 15. Rb1 Nxe3 16. Bxe3 Bxe3+ 17. Kh1 c6 18. Rbe1) } { [%cal Gb4c2,Ga1b1,Bc2e3,Bc1e3] }) (7... Bg4 8. exd4 Nxd4 9. Nbd2 O-O 10. h3 Bh5 11. g4 Bg6 12. Nxd4 exd4 13. Ne4) 8. exd4 exd4 9. Nbd2 Bg4 10. h3 Bh5 11. g4 Bg6 12. Nb3 { Black will have a hard time keeping the pawn unless they trade their bishop for a knight. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gf3d4,Gb3d4] }) 6. O-O Bd6 (6... Be7 7. b4 Bxb4 (7... Nxb4 8. Nxe5 O-O 9. a3 Na6 10. Bb2 Nc5 11. Nd2 Re8 12. Nef3 Ne6 13. Qc2 c5 14. e3) 8. Nxe5 Nxe5 9. Qa4+ { [%csl Gc6,Bb4][%cal Ge5c6,Gg2c6,Ba4b4] }) (6... Bg4 7. b4 Bxb4 8. Nxe5 Nxe5 9. Qa4+ { [%cal Ge5c6,Gg2c6,Ba4b4] }) (6... a5! { Best move for black - stops any b4 plan early. } { [%cal Rb2b4] } 7. e3 Bc5 (7... Be7 8. exd4 exd4 9. Na3 { With a5 played, we can go Nb5 without being quickly challenged. } { [%csl Gb5][%cal Ga3b5] } 9... O-O 10. Nb5 Bf5 11. Bf4 Rc8 12. Ne5 { Likely continuation: (12... Nxe5 13. Bxe5 c6 14. Nxd4 Bg6 15. Bh3) } { [%cal Gc6e5,Gf4e5,Bc7c6,Bb5d4] }) (7... dxe3 8. Bxe3 Be7 9. Nc3 O-O 10. Re1 Bg4 { Bf4 is just as common, we respond with d4: (10... Bf5 11. d4 exd4 12. Nxd4 Nxd4 13. Qxd4) } 11. h3 Bh5 12. g4 Bg6 13. d4 { Likely continuation: (13. d4 exd4 14. Nxd4 Nxd4 15. Qxd4) }) 8. exd4 Bxd4 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 10. Re1 O-O 11. h3 { We don't have time to capture the pawn - it gets dangerous: (11. Rxe5 Bg4 12. Qd2 Qd7 13. Nc3 Rae8 14. Re3 Bh3) } { [%cal Re1e5] } 11... Re8 12. Nd2 c6 13. Nb3 Bf5 14. Bd2 { We should be able to get the pawn back.
(14... Bxd3 15. Bxa5 Rxa5 16. Qxd3)
But the more critical continuation is: (14... Nxb3 15. axb3 Bxd3 16. Re3 e4 17. Qe1 Lining up on both pawns - black can't hold on to both 17... b6 18. Bc3 Planning to remove one defender of the pawn. 18... Qe7 19. Rd1 Rad8 20. Bxf6 Qxf6 21. Rdxd3) } { [%cal Gf5d3,Gd2a5] }) (6... h5 { [Rare 2%]
Looks dangerous but we need to be fearless and ignore the pawn. } 7. b4 Bxb4 8. Nxe5 Nxe5 9. Qa4+ Nc6 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. Qxb4 h4 12. Nd2 hxg3 13. fxg3 Ng4 14. Rf4) (6... h6 7. b4) 7. a3 a5 (7... O-O!? 8. b4 { Whenever black doesn't play a5 after a3 - we go with b4. } 8... a6 9. Nbd2 Bg4 10. h3 Bh5 11. c5 Be7 12. Bb2 Nd5 13. Qb3 { Setting up a sneaky trap } 13... Kh8 { [%cal Gf3e5] } 14. Nxe5 Nxe5 15. Qxd5) 8. e3 O-O 9. exd4 exd4 (9... Nxd4 10. Nxd4 exd4 11. Bg5 { With this move, we stop Bg4 which would be annoying.
This is why it's much better than Re1 (11. Re1 Bg4 12. Bf3) } 11... h6 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. Nd2 c6 14. Ne4 { Next we can capture the bishop and go Re1. } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Ge4d6,Bf1e1] }) 10. Re1 Bg4 11. h3 Bh5 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bxf6 Qxf6 14. Nbd2 Ne5 15. g4 Nxf3+ 16. Qxf3 Qxf3 17. Nxf3 Bg6 { [%cal Gc4c5] } 18. c5 Bxc5 19. Ne5 Bh7 20. Nd7 *
